# Enhanced Stability of Perovskite Solar Cells through Corrosion-Free Pyridine Derivatives in Hole-Transporting Materials

## abstract
The molecular structure of pyridine derivatives is critical to perovskite solar cell performance, especially stability. Most of the pyridine additives easily form complexes with perovskite. A new pyridine additive with a long alkyl chain substituted at its o-position does not corrode perovskite. The stability of devices containing this additive is the highest among the investigated cells.

Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) based on lead halide perovskite (e.g., $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ ) have recently attracted worldwide attention in the photovoltaic technology field because of their low costs and high efficiency.[1] The power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of these devices have increased from $\approx4.0\%$ to $22.0\%$ .[2] However, the large-scale application of highly efficient PSCs has been restricted by the poor long-term stability, which is induced by several complicated degradation processes, such as photoreaction at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface,[3] thermal-instability of perovskite,[4] iodide diffusion at bias,[5] water incursions or corrosion induced by additives in organic hole-transporting materials (HTMs).[6] For example, the reported highest efficiency to date was obtained with a PSC based on a mesoscopic structure, where additives such as lithium salts, cobalt complex, and 4-tertbutyl pyridine (TBP) were prevailingly used in HTMs to largely enhance the device efficiency. Lithium salts was found easily to absorb water, which can accelerate the moisture-induced decomposition of perovskite.[6b] In the case of decomposition by moisture, we expect that more advanced sealing techniques will circumvent this problem. However, for the case where additives like TBP corroding perovskite within the cell, there is a greater threat to the long-term stability. Currently very few reports had addressed the decomposition of perovskite in the presence of this additive and no facile solutions have been proposed to this problem.[7]
Herein, we fabricated mesoscopic PSCs with various pyridine-based additives in $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di-pmethoxyphenylamine)- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) and compared their photovoltaic performance with that of a TBP-based device. We observed that Lewis-base pyridine additives could strongly enhance the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs. We also confirmed that most of the investigated pyridine derivatives corroded perovskite due to the formation of new complexes with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . This corrosion phenomenon led to chemical decomposition of the perovskite and deteriorated the interface between the perovskite and the HTMs. Most importantly, we found a new additive of pyridine with a long alkyl chain substituted at the $\textit{o}$ -position chemically stable with no corrosion to perovskite, thereby substantially improving PSC stability.
We fabricated mesoscopic PSCs using spiro-OMeTAD with pyridine additives in the HTM on the top of perovskite capping layer (Figure 1a,b). The pyridine additives with various alkyl-chain substituents in different positions (Figure 1c) were selected because of the differences in basicity and space steric hindrance. The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the cells with different additives were measured under simulated $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ solar irradiance $(100\mathrm{~mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ (Figure 2; photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S1, Supporting Information). In mesoscopic PSCs, hysteresis is usually evident between the $J{-}V$ curves collected in the forward (from the short-circuit to the open-circuit) and reverse (from open-circuit to the short-circuit) modes, especially when they are collected at relatively short delay times: the hysteresis between the two scan directions in the PCEs for a representative solar cell was larger when the $J{-}V$ curves were collected at a delay time of $50~\mathrm{ms}$ than at $1000~\mathrm{{ms}}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information). For comparison, we show all of the $J{-}V$ curves collected during forward scans at $10~\mathrm{mV}$ voltage step and a delay time of $50~\mathrm{ms}$ based on aperture area $1.02\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ sized cells (Figure 2, Table S1, Supporting Information). All of the photovoltaic data presented here are based on a large-area cell $(>1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ because small-area devices are likely to induce measurement errors.[8] The average PCEs of the forward and reverse scan for devices with different additives were shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values of the cell fabricated with spiro-OMeTAD without any pyridine additive was low; this result is likely a consequence of the lower conductivity of the HTM stemming from the lower solubility of the lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide (Li-TFSI) salt in the absence of pyridine-based additives.[9] Among the devices fabricated using HTM with the four investigated pyridine additives, the fill factor (FF) and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values varied (Table S1, Supporting Information). Generally, in solar cells, the series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ reduces FF but does not affect $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ or $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ if it is not too high. The $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ values of the 2-amylpyridine (2-Py)- and 4-amylpyridine (4-Py)-based cells (5.93 and $5.86~\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ were the lowest; those of the TBP- and pyridinebased cells (7.27 and $7.77\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively) were lower than that with no additive (Table S1, Supporting Information).
Consequently, the FF values of the 2-Py- and 4-Py-based cells were higher than those of the TBP-based, pyridine-based, and no-additive cells. The reduced $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ of devices using pyridine additives might be related to the enhanced conductivity of the organic HTM caused by $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ induced $\mathrm{{\tt~p}}$ -doping.[10] We further measured the conductivity of the HTMs with different pyridine additives according to the previously described method.[11] The conductivity for 4-py and 2-py were $2.17\times10^{-5}$ and $2.03\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~s~cm^{-1}}$ , which is higher than that with TBP $(1.90\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{s}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1})}}$ and pyridine $(1.62\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{s}\thinspace{c m}^{-1})}$ . The result of high conductivity is consistent with that of high FF. We also checked the solubility of Li-TFSI in the presence of pyridine additives, which showed that 2-Py and 4-Py additives increased the solubility of Li-TFSI than that of TBP. The increased solubility might result in a more effective dissolution and dispersion of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ , which enhanced the doping effect and improved the conductivity in HTM. The shunt resistance $(R_{\mathrm{sh}})$ values were deduced from the slope of the $J{-}V$ curves near the short-circuit current and listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information).
The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energies of the spiro-OMeTAD with additives TBP, pyridine, 4-Py, and 2-Py were $-5.31$ , –5.40, $-5.42$ , and $-5.43\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The HOMO energy of pristine spiroOMeTAD was $-5.01$ eV (Figure S4, Supporting Information), consistent with previously reported values.[12] The doping of spiro-OMeTAD by Li-TFSI and cobalt complex shifted the HOMO energy to a much lower value, whereas only the pyridine-based additives did not affect the HOMO energy of spiroOMeTAD (Figure S4, Supporting Information). We attribute the difference in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values to the difference in the HOMO energies of the HTM because the loss of potential energy at the perovskite/HTM interface decreases the built-in potential in PSCs. The valence band of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ was reported as $5.44~\mathrm{eV}.^{[13]}$ A more favorable alignment between the valence band of perovskite and the HOMO energies of 4-Py- and 2-Pybased spiro-OMeTAD allows efficient hole transport without inducing excessive interface recombination. Therefore, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values of the 4-Py- and 2-Py-based cells were higher than those of the cells with TBP and pyridine. The incident photon–current conversion efficiency (IPCE) correlated well with the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values: the IPCE spectra were broad and flat at ${\approx}80\%$ between 400 and $750\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ from the IPCE data were $96\%-100\%$ of the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurement.
We measured the changes in photovoltaic properties of the PSCs with different pyridine additives in dark during long-term storage $({\approx}1000\ \mathrm{h})$ in a dry box. The dark condition can exclude other photo/thermo-induced degradation because here we focus on the effect of pyridine based additives to perovskite. As shown in Figure 3, the PCEs of the TBP-, pyridine-, and 4-Pybased cells decayed by from $5\%$ to $30\%$ during the first week, whereas that of the 2-Py-based device gradually increased and then remained at ${\approx}13\%$ after $900\mathrm{{h}}$ . Notably, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}},\:V_{\mathrm{OC}},$ or FF values of the cells with 2-Py did not decrease during the tests.
For other tested pyridine-based cells, a decrease in the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ was observed. More durability test of the 2-Py-based cells was performed to confirm this (Figure S6, Supporting Information). We also carried out stability investigation of the encapsulated devices with 2-Py and TBP-based cells under continuous light $(100\mathrm{\mW\cdotcm^{-2}},$ ). As shown in Figure S7 (Supporting Information), the devices with 2-Py additive shows enhanced stability, with $14\%$ decrease in the performance after $150\mathrm{~h~}$ , while the devices with TBP additives showed a decrease of $\approx50\%$ under the same aging condition. The degradation under light soaking could be due to a number of factors, such as photoreaction at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface or some subtle changes within perovskite under light. The simple encapsulation technique may also induce some degradation due to diffusion of reactants into the devices, but compared with other pyridine-based devices, 2-Py-based one showed obvious better device stability.
To investigate why stability varied among the cells, we used X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis to characterize their structures. To prepare the XRD samples, we dissolved the four pyridine additives separately in chlorobenzene at the same molar ratio used in the cells $(0.12\textrm{M})$ and then spin-coated the resulting solutions onto perovskite films in an $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -filled glove box (humidity: $<5~\mathrm{ppm}$ ). Here, we did not use other materials such as spiro-OMeTAD, Li-TFSI, cobalt, or acetonitrile to eliminate any effects due to these compounds. We collected the XRD patterns of freshly prepared samples and the same samples after storage in a dry box for 7 d (Figure 4). The main diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns of all of the perovskite films coated with the different additives were at $14.08^{\circ}$ , $28.41^{\circ}$ , and $31.85^{\circ}$ (Figure 4a), corresponding to the (110), (220), and (310) diffraction planes of perovskite, respectively. The crystal structure of all of the perovskite films prepared from $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ precursor solutions were tetragonal.[4] New peaks were observed at $\approx6.9\leq2\theta^{\circ}\leq7.5$ in the patterns of the films with TBP and pyridine additives after the samples were stored for $^\textrm{\scriptsize7d}$ under dry air (Figure 4b). These peaks $(6.9\leq2\theta^{\circ}\leq7.5)$ cannot be assigned to any perovskite or lead halide phases. Jeon et al. have reported similar peaks in the XRD pattern of a crystalline $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}-$ $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ –DMSO complex.[2d] In this work, pyridine additives such as TBP may be prone to forming complexes with perovskite, especially at the perovskite/HTM interface. Moreover, these peaks were not observed in the XRD patterns of the fresh films (Figure 4a), which indicates that the corrosion in the films is a kinetically slow process. The peaks appeared after the TBP additives gradually diffused to the perovskite layer and subsequently formed complexes. Manipulating and maintaining the interface between perovskite and HTM in PSCs are well known to be important for maximizing the electron and hole separation and for minimizing electron recombination.[2c] Such corrosion at the perovskite/HTM interface can substantially degrade the performance of PSCs, especially their long-term stability.
The corrosion might ensue via the following process (Figure 4c). Initially, pyridine additives existed mainly in the HTM layer or at the perovskite/HTM interface. As the molecules diffused to the perovskite layer, the perovskite/HTM interface and the grain boundaries became highly susceptible to attack and form complex. The size and steric hindrance of the substituents in the pyridine additives is one of the critical factors that affect the interaction between pyridine additives and perovskite. For 4-Py, an obvious peak at $6.9\leq2\theta^{\circ}\leq7.5$ was also found in XRD measurement when the film was kept for a longer time (Figure S8, Supporting Information). However, in the case of 2-Py, the corrosion peak was not observed. The space steric hindrance from the $o$ -substitution of pyridine ring may prevent the 2-Py forming complex with perovskite (Figure 4d).
To further investigate the complexes formed by the reaction of different pyridine additives with perovskite, we separately spin-coated one drop of each pure pyridine additive (no dilution) directly onto perovskite films in the glove box. Notably, all of the coated perovskite films immediately $(<1\ \mathrm{\s})$ changed color from dark brown to yellow, except for the perovskite film treated with 2-Py, which exhibited no color change (Figure 5a). All of the treated films were further characterized by XRD, scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy.
In the patterns of the films treated with pyridine, TBP, or 4-Py, the main perovskite XRD peaks, which are assigned to the (110), (220), and (310) diffraction planes, disappeared or weakened (Figure 5b); notably, however, the main peak associated with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(2\theta=12.67^{\circ})$ was not observed. These results indicate that the color change to pale yellow was not simply caused by the decomposition of the perovskite to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ but was rather a consequence of the formation of new phases with strong diffraction peaks at $6.9\leq2\theta^{\circ}\leq7.5$ . Surprisingly, no obvious change in the diffraction peaks was observed for the film treated with 2-Py (Figure 5b), which indicates that the perovskite sample treated with 2-Py retained its tetragonal crystal structure.
SEM top-view images of the complexes obtained from perovskite coated with the different pyridine additives further confirm this observation (Figure 5c): substantially different crystal structures were obtained in the samples treated with TBP, pyridine, and $4{\cdot}\mathrm{Py},$ whereas the morphology of the 2-pytreated sample was similar to that of the untreated perovskite. We performed FT-IR analysis to further characterize the new complexes. The FT-IR spectrum of the pristine perovskite crystals shows a pronounced N–H vibration between 3100 and $3200~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the presence of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ $(\mathrm{MA^{+}})$ in the perovskite; by contrast, these feature peaks disappeared in the spectra of samples treated with TBP, pyridine, and 4-Py (Figure S9, Supporting Information). These results indicate that the new complexes are mainly formed from $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and pyridine additives and that $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ (MAI) does not participate in the complexes. In order to confirm this, we directly synthesized the complexes by dissolving $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in TBP solution. The XRD pattern of the synthesized complex further confirmed the strong diffraction peak at around $7.0^{\circ}$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Therefore, the decomposition process of perovskite was speculated as follows
$$
\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}+x p y r i d i n e a d d i t i v e\leftrightarrow\mathrm{PbI_{2}}-\big(\mathrm{pyridineadditive}\big)x+\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}I}
$$
The pyridine additives react with perovskite to form new complexes, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}.$ –(pyridine additive) $x$ .[14] Here the decomposition process can involve multistep reactions, including pyridine additive induced perovskite dissociation, pyridine-lead coordination and decomposition of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ , etc.
As shown in the XRD patterns, SEM micrographs, and FT-IR spectra, the 2-Py additive did not react with perovskite, even when present in sufficient amounts, which indicates that it was stable with no corrosion to perovskite. Consistent with these observations, the stability of the device with 2-Py was the highest among the investigated devices. In the case of the sample treated with pyridine, many aggregated crystal structures were observed (Figure S11, Supporting Information), implying that the reaction between perovskite and pyridine was fast. In the case of the TBP-treated sample, some holes were observed in a homogeneous melt-like crystal structure, whereas in the case of the 4-Py-treated sample, some perovskite crystals remained on the surface (Figure S11, Supporting Information). These results further confirm the different reactivities of pyridine additives with perovskite; these reactivities are closely related to the long-term stability of PSCs. The stability of cells based on these additives decreases in the order 2-Py>>4-Py>TBP>pyridine.
Pyridine compounds, such as TBP have been frequently used as functional additives in organic HTMs for enhancing the device performance. However, the detailed function of pyridine additives as well as their impact on devise stability is largely unexplored. Our studies show that most of the pyridine additives can react with perovskite except 2-Py. The presence of alkyl chain at the $\phantom{-}o$ -position of pyridine ring in 2-Py is the key to modulate the reaction activity and the device stability. The alkyl chain near the nitrogen atom can decrease the coordination capability of pyridine, thus suppress the reaction with metal ions of lead.[15] In addition, this steric hindrance effect may also affect its diffusion to perovskite layer. These two effects sufficiently block the perovskite degradation in comparison with other pyridine additives. The TBP and other pyridine additives that seriously corroded perovskite, degraded the long-term stability of PSCs and should finally not be used in this application.
In summary, we observed that incorporating different pyridine additives in spiro-OMeTAD PSCs can dramatically improve photovoltaic performance; however, most Lewis-base pyridine additives can easily corrode perovskite by forming complexes because of their high binding affinity with the Lewis-acid $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , as confirmed by XRD, SEM, and FT-IR analyses. The typical XRD corrosion peaks at $6.9\leq2\theta^{\circ}\leq7.5$ of the new complexes are first reported. Most importantly, we found a new additive of pyridine with long alkyl chain substituted at the $\mid o\mid$ -position stable with no corrosion to the perovskite. PSCs based on this new pyridine additive exhibited improved stability compared to cells based on conventional TBP; this finding will facilitate the further development of efficient and highly stable PSCs. We hope these results facilitate better understanding of the perovskite material itself and advance its use in photovoltaic technology in the future.
## Experimental Section
Solar Cell Fabrication: A $30\ n m$ thick compact $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer was deposited onto fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass by spray pyrolysis. A $300\ \mathsf{n m}$ thick layer of mesoporous $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was spin-coated onto the compact $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer. The perovskite film was deposited via a one-step method from a DMSO solution of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ . The HTM was spin-coated at 3000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . The HTM solution was prepared by dissolving spiro-MeOTAD $770m g)$ , a stock solution of Li-TFSI in acetonitrile $(75~\upmu\up L$ $,170\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}},$ ), ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ complex (FK-102) in acetonitrile solution $58\upmu\up L$ , $300\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}},$ ), and pyridine-based additives $(0.12~\mathsf{M})$ in chlorobenzene $(2~\mathsf{m L})$ . The device fabrication was finally completed by thermal evaporation of a $\mathsf{100~n m}$ thick film of Au as the cathode.
Solar Cell Characterization: $J{-}V$ curves were collected using a digital source meter (2400, Keithley) under simulated AM $\mathsf{I}.5\mathrm{~C~}$ solar illumination of $100m w\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (WXS-90L2, Wacom).[16] The simulated light intensity was calibrated using a reference silicon photodiode. The reference cell was calibrated in the National Institute of Advanced Industrial Science and Technology (AIST), Japan. The devices were scanned from $-0.2$ to $+1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ (or $+1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ to $-0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ for the reverse scan) with a voltage step of $\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{10~mV}}}$ and a delay time of $50~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{s}$ at room temperature. IPCE data were collected with monochromatic incident light of $10^{16}$ photons $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ in DC mode (CEP-2000, BunkoKeiki). The active area of all the prepared devices is $\approx1.27~{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}$ . All of the $J{-}V$ and IPCE measurements were conducted using a nonreflective metal mask with an aperture area of $\mathsf{1.02\ c m^{2}}$ to cover the active area of the device. The $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ value dose not varies significantly with the aperture area.
Further information on materials and the full processes of solar cell fabrication, characterization, and stability test are available in the Supporting Information.
